
==========================================================================
STEP 0: build the topology
==========================================================================
  switch ports: h1=1, h2=2, h3=3, h4=4
  h1 (client), s1 (OpenFlow 1.3 switch), h2/h3/h4 (servers) are up.
  the client asks for 10.0.0.100, which all three servers own as a /32.

==========================================================================
STEP 1: the flow table is empty, so the switch drops everything
==========================================================================
  s1 has no rules and no controller.  Its fail mode is "secure", which
  means unmatched packets are dropped instead of being forwarded.
  The client will ask for the VIP and get nothing back.
  --- OpenFlow rules currently in s1 (expected to be empty) ---
  FAIL no-rules     rule->-   served_by=-    2506.6 ms  (TimeoutError)
  -> no answer, exactly as expected: without rules there is no service.

==========================================================================
STEP 2: install the forwarding rules, traffic starts to flow
==========================================================================
  Two kinds of rule are added:
    * one per host, so replies reach it by MAC address
    * one that floods ARP, so hosts can find each other
  Then the important one: VIP traffic is sent to h2.
  OK   baseline     rule->h2  served_by=h2     11.6 ms
  --- OpenFlow rules currently in s1 (after one request: the VIP rule has forwarded packets) ---
     cookie=0x0, duration=1.019s, table=0, n_packets=6, n_bytes=481, priority=100,ip,nw_dst=10.0.0.100 actions=set_field:00:00:00:00:00:11->eth_dst,output:2
     cookie=0x0, duration=1.199s, table=0, n_packets=6, n_bytes=603, priority=50,dl_dst=00:00:00:00:00:01 actions=output:1
     cookie=0x0, duration=1.163s, table=0, n_packets=1, n_bytes=42, priority=50,dl_dst=00:00:00:00:00:11 actions=output:2
     cookie=0x0, duration=1.136s, table=0, n_packets=0, n_bytes=0, priority=50,dl_dst=00:00:00:00:00:12 actions=output:3
     cookie=0x0, duration=1.108s, table=0, n_packets=0, n_bytes=0, priority=50,dl_dst=00:00:00:00:00:13 actions=output:4
     cookie=0x0, duration=1.081s, table=0, n_packets=1, n_bytes=42, priority=10,arp actions=FLOOD

==========================================================================
STEP 3: monitor the servers with a health check
==========================================================================
  From h1 we open a plain TCP connection to each server's own address
  (10.0.0.11, 10.0.0.12, 10.0.0.13 - not the VIP).  A server that
  answers is healthy, and its connect time is a latency sample we can
  use for selection.  The VIP rule plays no part in this at all.
  --- health check (baseline: all three servers should be UP) ---
     h2 10.0.0.11  UP       1.6 ms
     h3 10.0.0.12  UP       4.4 ms
     h4 10.0.0.13  UP       4.2 ms

==========================================================================
STEP 4: select the server from health + latency, not round-robin
==========================================================================
  Policy: only healthy servers are candidates, and among them the one
  with the lowest measured latency is chosen.  Each round probes,
  decides, re-points the VIP rule, then sends one request, so the
  reason for the choice is printed before the traffic moves.
  (The three virtual links are identical, so their latencies differ
  only by noise and the same server tends to win again and again.
  That is the correct result for this policy; step 5 keeps the
  round-robin pass precisely to show traffic can be spread too.)
  --- health check (before request 1 of 6) ---
     h2 10.0.0.11  UP       0.3 ms
     h3 10.0.0.12  UP       0.2 ms
     h4 10.0.0.13  UP       0.2 ms
  decision: healthy=h2,h3,h4, lowest latency=h3 -> VIP -> h3
  OK   smart        rule->h3  served_by=h3      7.9 ms
  --- health check (before request 2 of 6) ---
     h2 10.0.0.11  UP       0.3 ms
     h3 10.0.0.12  UP       0.2 ms
     h4 10.0.0.13  UP       0.2 ms
  decision: healthy=h2,h3,h4, lowest latency=h3 -> VIP -> h3
  OK   smart        rule->h3  served_by=h3      1.7 ms
  --- health check (before request 3 of 6) ---
     h2 10.0.0.11  UP       0.2 ms
     h3 10.0.0.12  UP       0.1 ms
     h4 10.0.0.13  UP       0.2 ms
  decision: healthy=h2,h3,h4, lowest latency=h3 -> VIP -> h3
  OK   smart        rule->h3  served_by=h3      1.8 ms
  --- health check (before request 4 of 6) ---
     h2 10.0.0.11  UP       0.2 ms
     h3 10.0.0.12  UP       0.2 ms
     h4 10.0.0.13  UP       0.1 ms
  decision: healthy=h2,h3,h4, lowest latency=h4 -> VIP -> h4
  OK   smart        rule->h4  served_by=h4      5.6 ms
  --- health check (before request 5 of 6) ---
     h2 10.0.0.11  UP       0.2 ms
     h3 10.0.0.12  UP       0.1 ms
     h4 10.0.0.13  UP       0.2 ms
  decision: healthy=h2,h3,h4, lowest latency=h3 -> VIP -> h3
  OK   smart        rule->h3  served_by=h3      1.8 ms
  --- health check (before request 6 of 6) ---
     h2 10.0.0.11  UP       0.2 ms
     h3 10.0.0.12  UP       1.0 ms
     h4 10.0.0.13  UP       0.3 ms
  decision: healthy=h2,h3,h4, lowest latency=h2 -> VIP -> h2
  OK   smart        rule->h2  served_by=h2      1.7 ms
