==========================================================================
STEP 6: throughput, one 8 MiB download per server
==========================================================================
  Same mechanism, bigger file: the VIP rule chooses who sends it.
  The numbers are real bytes per second, but between virtual hosts they
  mostly measure memory speed rather than a real network link.
  OK   rule->h2 served_by=h2 8388621 bytes in   28.0 ms =  299.4 MB/s
  OK   rule->h3 served_by=h3 8388621 bytes in   43.3 ms =  193.7 MB/s
  OK   rule->h4 served_by=h4 8388621 bytes in   26.1 ms =  322.1 MB/s

==========================================================================
STEP 7: a path failure - unplug h3's link at the switch
==========================================================================
  h3's web server keeps running and its address is unchanged; only the
  cable is pulled.  Compare the error with step 8: a broken path cannot
  answer at all, so the request times out instead of being refused.
  FAIL path-failure rule->h3  served_by=-    2503.1 ms  (TimeoutError)
  --- health check (h3 should be DOWN: its path is gone) ---
     h2 10.0.0.11  UP       0.5 ms
     h3 10.0.0.12  DOWN   401.2 ms
     h4 10.0.0.13  UP       0.5 ms
  the policy drops h3 and keeps serving on the other two:
  OK   path-failure rule->h4  served_by=h4      3.2 ms
  OK   path-failure rule->h4  served_by=h4      1.6 ms
  OK   path-failure rule->h4  served_by=h4      2.0 ms
  OK   path-failure rule->h4  served_by=h4      2.1 ms
  plug the cable back in:
  --- health check (h3 should be UP again) ---
     h2 10.0.0.11  UP       0.3 ms
     h3 10.0.0.12  UP       0.7 ms
     h4 10.0.0.13  UP       0.2 ms
  OK   path-back    rule->h3  served_by=h3      1.9 ms

==========================================================================
STEP 8: a server failure - kill h3's web server
==========================================================================
  This time the path is fine and the process dies.  On purpose the VIP
  rule is left pointing at h3, so the next request must fail: that is
  what a dead server looks like to a user.
  FAIL failure      rule->h3  served_by=-       0.4 ms  (ConnectionRefusedError)
  -> refused, not timed out: the link is fine, the process is gone.
  Now the health check runs.  h3 refuses the probe, so it is marked
  DOWN.  Nothing in the code mentions h3: the exclusion comes from the
  measurement.
  --- health check (h3 should be DOWN) ---
     h2 10.0.0.11  UP       0.3 ms
     h3 10.0.0.12  DOWN     0.1 ms
     h4 10.0.0.13  UP       9.4 ms

==========================================================================
STEP 9: availability - keep serving while h3 is unhealthy
==========================================================================
  Requests keep arriving while h3 is dead.  Because the policy only
  offers healthy servers, every request is served.  The client is never
  told anything and still asks for the same VIP address.
  OK   availability rule->h2  served_by=h2      1.8 ms
  OK   availability rule->h2  served_by=h2      1.6 ms
  OK   availability rule->h2  served_by=h2      1.6 ms
  OK   availability rule->h2  served_by=h2      1.6 ms
  OK   availability rule->h2  served_by=h2      1.7 ms
  OK   availability rule->h2  served_by=h2      1.9 ms
  --- OpenFlow rules currently in s1 (while h3 is dead the VIP rule only ever points at h2 or h4) ---
